index=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Daily Ingest — 2026-09-30</title>{CSS}</head><body><main><header><div class="wordmark">{MARK}</div><div class="eyebrow">Adaptive Daily Ingest · 2026-09-30</div><h1>From always-on agents to governed context</h1><p class="readout">Today’s dossier follows one practical thesis: agent capability is arriving as an operating layer, but durable leverage depends on three seams—cost-aware execution, security control planes, and portable knowledge/context.</p></header><section class="card"><h2>Selected lanes</h2><div class="grid">{''.join(f'<div class="node"><strong>{html.escape(v[0])}</strong><p>{html.escape(v[1][:180])}…</p><a href="{k}.html">Open lane →</a></div>' for k,v in datasets.items())}</div></section><section class="card top"><h2>Top three today</h2><ol><li>Benchmark GPT-6.1 Sol on real long-horizon tasks with cost and retry telemetry.</li><li>Build an agent inventory and convert the security baseline into release gates.</li><li>Add deprecation monitoring and contract tests before expanding agent interfaces.</li></ol></section><section class="card"><h2>Editorial note</h2><p>Coverage deliberately avoids repeating yesterday’s runtime-control, privacy-by-construction, and agent-architecture lanes. The new emphasis is on adoption surfaces and the operational seams needed to make them safe and portable.</p></section><footer>Static HTML · no scripts · <a href="ledger.json">source ledger</a></footer></main></body></html>'''
